data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider deprecated synchronous LINQ execution but allowed it via an opt-in flag (`EnableSyncExecution` / sync-over-async bridging). **In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed.** Synchronous query operators (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, etc.) and `SaveChanges()` against the Cosmos provider now throw `InvalidOperationException` — there is no workaround other than going async.

## What to change

Convert all data-access methods to their async counterparts:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);   // Add() is in-memory, stays sync
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- `Add()` is an in-memory tracker operation — it remains synchronous and is fine.
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()` — it hits the network.
- All LINQ terminal operators (`ToList`, `First`, `Count`, `Any`, `Single`, etc.) must use their `*Async` variants.
- Callers of these methods will also need to be updated to `await` the results, so expect the async change to ripple up through controllers/services.
